  "full_response": "### **Zero-Trust Security Architecture: An Overview**\n\nZero-Trust is a modern cybersecurity framework that assumes **no user, device, or system—inside or outside an organization’s network—should be trusted by default**, even if they are already inside the corporate perimeter. Instead of relying on traditional perimeter-based security (like firewalls and VPNs), Zero-Trust enforces strict identity verification, least-privilege access, and continuous monitoring for every access request.\n\n---\n\n## **Core Principles of Zero-Trust**\nThe Zero-Trust model is built on the following key principles:\n\n### **1. Never Trust, Always Verify**\n- Every access request (user, device, or application) must be **authenticated, authorized, and encrypted** before granting access.\n- No entity is inherently trusted, regardless of location (inside or outside the network).\n\n### **2. Least-Privilege Access**\n- Users and systems are granted the **minimum permissions necessary** to perform their tasks.\n- Reduces the risk of lateral movement if an account is compromised.\n\n### **3. Assume Breach (Micro-Segmentation)**\n- Operates under the assumption that **attackers are already inside the network**.\n- **Micro-segmentation** divides the network into small, isolated zones to limit lateral movement.\n- Example: A database server should only communicate with specific application servers, not the entire network.\n\n### **4. Continuous Authentication & Monitoring**\n- **Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)** is required for all access attempts.\n- **Behavioral analytics** and **real-time monitoring** detect anomalies (e.g., unusual login locations, data exfiltration attempts).\n- Access is **continuously re-evaluated**—if suspicious activity is detected, access is revoked.\n\n### **5. Device & Application Security**\n- **All devices** (corporate or BYOD) must meet security standards (e.g., up-to-date patches, endpoint protection).\n- **Applications** are verified before allowing access (e.g., via app-level encryption, API gateways).\n\n### **6. Encryption Everywhere**\n- **Data is encrypted in transit and at rest** to prevent interception or theft.\n- Even internal communications are secured (e.g., TLS for all traffic).\n\n---\n\n## **How Zero-Trust Works: Key Components**\nA Zero-Trust architecture typically includes the following elements:\n\n| **Component**          | **Description** |\n|------------------------|----------------|\n| **Identity & Access Management (IAM)** | Uses **",
